    title: 'The Autonomy of Art',
    passageText: `The question of whether art is autonomous — whether it can be understood and evaluated independently of the social, political, and biographical circumstances of its production — has divided aestheticians since the Romantic period. The autonomy thesis holds that aesthetic value is intrinsic: the work of art contains within itself the resources for its own appreciation and evaluation. To properly understand a painting or a poem, one need only attend to the work itself — its formal properties, internal coherence, and achieved emotional effects — rather than to who painted it, when, or under what political conditions.

The autonomy thesis has intuitive appeal: great works of literature can be appreciated across centuries and cultures, suggesting that aesthetic value transcends its historical occasion. It also supports the practice of formalism — close reading of textual or visual properties without recourse to contextual information — which has been an extraordinarily productive critical methodology.

Critics of autonomy argue that the very concept of the "work itself" is an ideological construction. The formal properties of a work are not neutral facts but historically conditioned perceptions shaped by conventions, institutions, and interpretive traditions. To understand Beethoven's Ninth Symphony purely in terms of its musical structure is to suppress its complex social reception history, including its use by both revolutionary and totalitarian political movements. Art, on this view, is constitutively social — it cannot be stripped of its context without distortion.

A middle position suggests that these perspectives are not exclusive. Works of art may be understood at multiple levels simultaneously: as formal constructions amenable to immanent analysis and as social documents that bear the traces of their historical production. The art-historical method combines formal analysis with contextual research precisely because neither approach alone gives a complete picture. The question is not whether context matters but how much weight it should receive in specific interpretive decisions.`,
